 over the north american free trade  agreement  between vice president al gore and ross  perot was watched by 11 million people  at the time a record for cable  television born larry ziegler  king changed his name when he began his  radio career in the 1950s  he survived a heart attack in 1987 and  was married eight times  to seven different women after stepping  down from larry king live in 2010  king had second thoughts about  retirement and took a show to the  internet  debuting larry king now in 2012. i love  asking questions  i'm very much into people and you never  lose that  larry king a radio and television icon  he asked the questions americans wanted  to ask  dan schenemann nbc news  still ahead the revolutionary technology  decades in the making  that is so crucial to cover 19 vaccines  you're watching global national  on this day last year 11 million people  in wuhan china  entered the world's first coveted 19  lockdown those long  76 days were key to keeping the virus in  check  no cases have been reported there since  may and life has mostly returned to  normal  but in hong kong an outbreak of coven 19  has triggered the city's first ever  lockdown  the order covers the neighborhood of  jordan in kowloon peninsula  thousands of people must stay home for  48 hours and every resident in that zone  will be tested for the virus  the two vaccines approved for use in  canada have a  fascinating backstory they use a genetic  technology scientists have been working  on  for years it's called messenger rna or  mrna they knew it held promise and when  19 hit they ramped up research and  cracked the code  the results are the first mrna vaccines  approved for use  in humans and as donna friesen explains  the technology could revolutionize the  field of vaccines  zara kahia spends her days caring for  the vulnerable  she's a respiratory therapist in the  chronic ventilator unit  at capital care norwood in edmonton they  have  compromised respiratory systems and they  require  24-hour respiratory support when she  heard the pfizer biontec and moderna  vaccines were approved  she had questions i'm a numbers girl so  i tended to kind of read the studies and  that kind of  made me feel more confident in taking  this vaccine  they are mrna vaccines technology that  is revolutionary  but not entirely new scientists have  been working on it for decades  it's a very precise and elegant way  of of telling our immune system to  respond against  the virus this in simple terms is how  mrna vaccines work  the coronavirus's spike protein which  forms its crown  is what makes it so infectious mrna  vaccines work by  using that weapon against the virus  itself  like a computer code synthetic mrna  tells the cells of the body  how to make the spike protein scientists  had to find a way to inject it  without having the immune system  immediately attack it  they came up with a lipid nanoparticle a  kind of invisibility cloak  a fatty bubble that encapsulates the  mrna  and keeps it from being destroyed  allowing it to slip  into the cell once inside the mrna  is released and the cell starts building  the spike protein  mimicking a covid19 infection it is  completely harmless  but it triggers the immune system into  creating antibodies against the  coronavirus  it is a breakthrough that's advanced  work already underway on other vaccines  and treatment for other diseases  including cancer  kahia was the first person in alberta to  get the covet vaccine  the first question is are you okay and  then the second question  is should i get this vaccine and i  always answer with yes  donna friesen global news to learn more  about how the kobit 19 mrna vaccines  work  watch donna's entire story tonight on  the new reality  right here on global value village  riches up next what employees did when  they found a treasure at a second-hand  store  this next story begs the question what  would you do  if this happened to you employees at a  vancouver thrift store were sorting  through donation bags when they came  across  a hidden treasure 85 thousand dollars in  cash  and as nadia stewart reports it's what  happened next  that has everyone talking it is the  place where people go to get more bang  for their buck  thrifty deals without breaking the bank  but what staff found when sorting  through donations this week  was an unusual find to say the least  holy smokes  this is a lot of money jeffrey  stonehouse manages a value village in  vancouver  days ago he and another staffer were  sorting through a pile of donations  when they came across a bag that was  full of surprises  the bag was a little unusual it felt  like documents  and so she pulled out the first envelope  and tore the top off of it  and pointed it in my direction and what  we saw was a lot of  fifties and hundred dollar bills i don't  know how old this money is  i can tell you that the money had been  stowed away for quite  some time stone house says  even police were taken aback by the  amount over 85  thousand dollars the cash was in a bag a  dollar store bag no less  and a contained id that helped police  identify the elderly woman whose cash  this was  as it turns out there's an elderly woman  she's now living in a care home she's  been in the care home for about a year  and her family was going through some  old storage lockers that she had and  they found some bags of stuff  they decided to donate the bags to value  village  not obviously knowing what was in it and  never knowing that this money even  existed  the money is now on its way back to the  family with police praising  value village staff along the way they  did the right thing  they called the police stonehouse says  what he and his staff did  was what they always do when receiving  something they feel  was not intended for them usually it's  when we get a call from somebody they've  misdonated something  it's usually like my wedding dress or my  grad photos or something like that  but this certainly was outstanding this  was an unintended donation  they won't soon forget nadia stewart  global news vancouver and that is global  national for this saturday  i'm robin gill tonight your canada is  the hamlet of beaver creek saskatchewan  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night
 

